Step 3. For each field that needs protection we “lock in” the drones already protecting it. Thus:
 – Field_3 keeps drones 16, 20, 37, 39.
 – Field_7 already has drones 34 and 35 (needs one more).
 – Field_9 is full but its threat (0.08) is low so we “release” its drones (2, 3, 17) to be re‐used for fields with higher threat.
 – Field_5 is not a concern (threat 0.00) so we reassign its drones (19, 33) for higher threats.

Step 4. Now we add drones “from the available pool” (including those in “moving_to_field” or idle, reassigned if coming from a low–threat field) to cover the deficits of the highest–threat fields. Because our overall drone fleet is limited (40 total) we make the following reassignments so that in high–priority fields we have exactly the numbers required for full protection. (We prefer drones that are “closest” by already‐given location data; we simulate a “closest–by–group” selection here.)

