Step 2. We decide to protect only the fields that are most threatened because there are not enough drones to cover every field. In descending order the highest threat fields are:
 – Field_10 (threat 0.23)
 – Field_15 (threat 0.23)
 – Field_11 (threat 0.20)
 – Field_14 (threat 0.20)
Then (in descending order) fields such as Field_12, Field_18, Field_16, Field_17 would be next. In our assignment we assume that with the available moving drones we concentrate on the very highest‐threat fields. In addition, the fields that are already over‐protected (Field_4, Field_5, Field_7, Field_8) are left unchanged. (Any field that is not a high priority or already fully covered will have no additional drones assigned – those drones originally heading there will be repurposed to the highest-threat fields.)

Step 3. For each high‐threat field already “protecting” drones remain on “protecting” their field.
 • Field_4 keeps drones 4, 5, 7, 25, 27, 39.
 • Field_5 keeps drones 1, 2, 11, 13, 14, 16.
 • Field_7 keeps drones 17, 34, 35, 38.
 • Field_8 keeps drones 21, 30.

Step 4. Using the pool of moving-to drones (the 22 drones whose state is “moving_to_field”) we reassign them by comparing their locations to the “centers” of the high‑priority fields. For the purpose of our calculations we determine approximate field centers as follows:
 • Field_10: center ≈ (41,100)
