Step 1. For every field we first list the “already protecting” drones (that is, those whose state is “protecting”) and based on each field’s “for full protection” requirement we compute how many extra drones would be needed if we wanted to cover it fully.
• Field_1 (threat = 0.14; full protection requires 4): Already protecting are Drone_4, Drone_20, Drone_37 and Drone_40. (4 drones – so no additional drone is needed.)
• Field_2 (threat = 0.07; full protection requires 2): No drone is “protecting” it even though one drone (Drone_7) is already flying to it. (Needs 2 extra if protection were to be assigned.)
• Field_3 (threat = 0.07; full protection requires 4): No drone is protecting it. (Needs 4.)
• Field_4 (threat = 0.10; full protection requires 4): Already protecting is Drone_5. There are several drones flying toward Field_4 (Drone_25, Drone_27, Drone_31, Drone_33, and Drone_39). In a full‐protection plan we would want a total of 4 protecting drones so we need 3 additional drones; later we choose the closest three among those headed for Field_4.
• Field_5 (threat = 0.00; full protection requires 2): Even though one drone (Drone_6) is “protecting” and five are “flying to” Field_5, the threat level is zero, so we decide not to devote resources here.
• Field_6 (threat = 0.10; full protection requires 4): No drone is “protecting” yet (but Drone_21 and Drone_30 are already in flight). So if we were to protect it we would need 2 additional drones.
• Field_7 (threat = 0.02; full protection requires 3): Two drones (Drone_17 and Drone_34) are protecting it. (Needs 1 extra, but because its threat is very low we prefer not to use scarce drones here.)
• Field_8 (threat = 0.01; full protection requires 2): No protection; threat is very low.
• Field_9 (threat = 0.04; full protection requires 3): No protection.
• Field_10 (threat = 0.16; full protection requires 6): No drone is onsite yet. (Needs 6.)
• Field_11 (threat = 0.21; full protection requires 4): No drone is protecting it. (Needs 4.)
• Field_12 (threat = 0.12; full protection requires 1): No protecting drone; needs 1.
• Field_13 (threat = 0.05; full protection requires 2): No protection.
• Field_14 (threat = 0.24; full protection requires 6): No protection; needs 6.
• Field_15 (threat = 0.22; full protection requires 6): No protection; needs 6.
• Field_16 (threat = 0.12; full protection requires 2): No protection; needs 2.
• Field_17 (threat = 0.13; full protection requires 2): No protection; needs 2.
• Field_18 (threat = 0.02; full protection requires 1): No protection.
• Field_19 (threat = 0.00; full protection requires 2): Threat zero so no action.
• Field_20 (threat = 0.08; full protection requires 3): Already protecting is Drone_2 while several drones (Drone_3, Drone_14, Drone_15, Drone_16 and Drone_36) are flying there. (Thus by “catching” two from those in flight we can have full protection.)

Step 2. Because there are not enough drones to cover every field (and low‐threat fields need not be protected immediately) we choose to protect only the most threatened fields. In our plan we decide to keep the already–protecting fields intact (Field_1 is covered) and then devote our “free” drones to the most important cases. We rank by threat level: Field_14 (0.24) and Field_15 (0.22) top the list, followed by Field_10 (0.16), Field_11 (0.21), Field_17 (0.13), Field_12 (0.12) and Field_16 (0.12). We also include Field_20 (0.08) and Field_6 (0.10) in the plan. However, since resources are scarce we decide to “fully protect” only the high‐threat fields whose additional need sums to 25 drones; given the 40 available drones and counting our pre–assigned ones (a total of 17 drones already “protecting” or “in flight” for chosen fields) we have 23 drones to allocate additionally. We then allocate the available extra drones in order of threat priority. In our allocation the very low–threat fields (Field_2, Field_3, Field_7, Field_8, Field_9, Field_13 and Field_18) as well as Field_5 and Field_19 are not assigned extra protection. Among the fields we do choose to protect fully, we give full protection (i.e. enough drones to “protect” the field) to Field_14, Field_15, Field_10, Field_11, Field_12, Field_16 and Field_17. Field_20 already comes to 3 with a mix of one already protecting and two chosen from in–flight drones. For Field_6 (threat = 0.10) we leave it partially covered with only the 2 drones in flight because its threat is lower than those we fully cover.

Step 3. For each field that needs protection we keep the drones already “protecting” the field. For example, Field_1 keeps its four drones; Field_4 already has Drone_5; Field_20 keeps Drone_2; Field_11 gets no “protecting” drone from the start so we must add from the free pool.

Step 4. For every field that needs additional drones we “add” drones chosen from those free (either currently “flying to” a field that is being cleared for protection or idle) by selecting the ones closest (by Euclidean distance from the field’s center) to the field. For instance, for Field_4 we select among the drones already moving toward it (and available) the three closest (Drone_25, Drone_27 and Drone_31). Similarly we choose the six closest drones from our free pool to assign to Field_14, and so on. (Note that drones originally slated for a non–protected field are re–assigned as “idle” if that field is not getting protection.)
When the free pool is “budget–limited” (23 drones available to cover an extra requirement of 25) we allocate the available 23 in full to the highest–threat fields – in our plan that ends up with Field_14, Field_15, Field_10, Field_11, Field_12, Field_16 and Field_17 receiving enough drones while Field_6 remains at its initial two.

Step 5. Final group assignments are made according to the allowed groups. (Any drone that was initially “moving to” a field that we decide not to protect – or that is in excess of what is needed – is instead assigned to “idle”.)

Below is the final assignment. Drones that were “pre–assigned” and are not needed (for example, those moving to fields that we now decide not to protect, or extra ones from low–priority fields) are reassigned to “idle.”

The final chosen assignments are as follows:
• Field_1 remains protected by its four drones.
• Field_4 is protected by the already protecting Drone_5 and by Drone_25, Drone_27 and Drone_31 chosen from those flying in.
• Field_6 keeps its two drones (Drone_21 and Drone_30) even though full protection would require four.
• Field_20 is protected by the already protecting Drone_2 plus we choose two from those flying (Drone_3 and Drone_14) to reach three.
• Field_11 gets four drones chosen from free (Drone_11, Drone_12, Drone_23, Drone_32).
• Field_10 receives six drones from the free pool (Drone_29, Drone_17, Drone_34, Drone_33, Drone_35 and Drone_36) because of its high threat level.
• Field_14 (the highest–threat field) gets six drones from free (Drone_7, Drone_1, Drone_22, Drone_18, Drone_28 and Drone_39) based on proximity.
• Field_15 (also very high threat) is assigned six drones from free (Drone_15, Drone_10, Drone_16, Drone_9, Drone_6 and Drone_8).
• Field_17 is assigned two drones from free (Drone_13 and Drone_19).
• Field_12 is given one drone from free (Drone_38).
• Field_16 is covered by two drones from free (Drone_24 and Drone_26).
All other drones – including those originally assigned to fly to fields we have now decided not to protect (for instance, Field_2, Field_3, Field_5, Field_7, Field_8, Field_9, Field_13, Field_18, Field_19) or those in excess – become “idle.”

